Palm rejection as macOS does it: by where a touch lands, not by whether keys
are held, and without stopping the touchpad while typing.

Upstream, a key held down keeps the keyboard "active" (a game's W turns the
touchpad off for as long as it is held), every touch that begins while typing
is a palm whatever it is, and the pointer, scrolling and gestures stop
outright until typing ends. Here only key presses count as typing, fingers
keep moving, scrolling and gesturing while keys are pressed (only taps wait),
and hands at rest are told apart by place, as measured over 945 touches on
this MacBook: the heel of a hand lands within 3 % of either side (deliberate
fingers start no nearer than 7 %), sometimes two seconds after the last key,
and a resting thumb in the bottom 2 %. So a touch beginning in the outer 5 %
either side within 2.5 s of a key press, or in the bottom 4 % at any time, is
PALM_REST: ignored for the pointer, taps, clickfinger and gestures until it
travels 4 mm (resting hands moved under 2), when it is a finger reaching in.

That also covers thumbs, so libinput's own thumb detection is off: on this
pad its thumb area and Apple's contact size threshold jailed ordinary
fingers, a hand on the pad in a game above all, which then never moved the
pointer.

